# MANAGED, NOT create_. This file used to be create_harness.toml.tmpl —
# seed-once-then-never-touch — which meant every edit here reached new machines
# only. tars' copy sat frozen at 2026-07-26 while this template moved on, so its
# harness names and profiles had drifted completely out of sync and no amount of
# `czu` would reconcile them. Verified before switching: harness does NOT rewrite
# this file (untouched through weeks of heavy use), so the create_ escape hatch
# was not buying anything — the daemon keeps its runtime state, including the
# active profile, in ~/.local/state/harness/state.json instead.
#
# Consequence to know: this template is now authoritative and an apply
# OVERWRITES the target. Do not hand-edit ~/.config/harness/harness.toml; the
# next czu reverts it.
#
# The daemon does not yet re-read on change (stump.wtf/harness#98), so a config
# that arrives via czu needs `harness reload` — or a daemon restart — to take
# effect. Until that lands, changing this file is two steps, not one.
# harness.toml — supervised long-running agents, managed end-to-end by chezmoi.
#
# Caveat that survives from the create_ era: harness's own TUI rewrites this
# file wholesale (internal/tui/form.go → os.WriteFile) when you add or edit a
# harness in the new-harness form. Those edits are TARGET-side, so the next
# `chezmoi apply` reverts them. Add a harness here, in the template — not in the
# TUI — or the change lives exactly until the next czu.
#
# Full field reference: ~/.local/share/harness-src/harness.toml.example and
# `harness daemon -h`. Fields not used here: tmux_socket and backend (defaults
# to "native", which runs the process under the daemon's own PTY per ADR-0003 —
# that's what a full-screen TUI like crush or claude needs; "tmux" is the compat
# escape hatch).
#
# RESTART POLICY. Every harness below sets `restart` and `restart_delay`
# explicitly. Both previously relied on defaults, and the defaults are a trap
# for an agent you drive from your phone:
#
#   restart       unset → "always" (fine, but pinned here so a future upstream
#                 default change cannot silently retire our supervision)
#   restart_delay unset → 0 — respawn INSTANTLY on exit
#
# A zero delay is what turns one bad afternoon into a dead agent. The daemon's
# crash-loop policy (internal/supervisor/policy.go DefaultPolicy) is 3 exits
# inside a 10s window → flapping, then escalating backoff, then FAILED after 5
# attempts — and `failed` is terminal, needing a human. With delay 0 a
# fast-failing crush burns all five attempts in about half a minute and is gone
# until someone runs `harness start`, from a phone, having had no notification.
#
# That paragraph describes the give-up rule as it WAS, and the rule has since
# changed. Read this half.
#
# Give-up used to be gated on `flapping`, which only trips on 3 exits inside
# the 10s crash window. Spacing retries wider than that window (delay = 5)
# therefore made give-up UNREACHABLE, and this note used to record that as a
# deliberate trade: retry forever, because for a phone-driven agent
# self-healing beats an error state nobody is at a desk to read.
#
# It was not a trade, it was the outage. A harness failing reliably but slowly
# outlived the crash window on every run, reset the counter every time, and
# reported `flapping no` throughout — while restarting 6,212 times and
# exhausting the provider's weekly quota. harness fixed it upstream by giving
# consecutive-failure accounting its own clock: a run clears the counter only
# by exiting cleanly or by lasting HealthyRun (5 min), and MaxRestarts (5) is
# checked against THAT (supervisor.go, "Consecutive-failure accounting").
#
# So a broken harness now parks in `failed` after ~6 attempts whatever
# restart_delay says, which is what we wanted all along. Two holes remain, and
# only the first is fixable from this file:
#
#   1. `restart = "always"` respawns on a CLEAN exit too, and a clean exit
#      CLEARS the failure counter — so an exit-0 loop is the one loop give-up
#      can never break. Use "on-failure" for anything that costs money per
#      launch; see crush-signal below.
#   2. A harness that runs LONGER than HealthyRun before dying resets the
#      counter every cycle and loops indefinitely. Nothing here can change
#      that — it is daemon policy — and for a metered agent each cycle is a
#      full session's tokens. Watch the provider's dashboard, not
#      `harness list`.
#
# Every agent below runs with permission prompts off, so all are
# `enabled = false`: the daemon will NOT autostart them on boot. Start one
# deliberately with `harness start <name>`. All also inherit the daemon's
# environment (spawn.go does `env := os.Environ()` and appends env_file on top).
# The daemon itself is supervised — systemd --user `harness.service` on Linux,
# LaunchAgent `rocks.stump.harness` on macOS (ADR-0005) — and both units load
# ~/.config/vault/secrets-static.env before exec, so every provider and MCP
# token reaches the agents without a login shell in the loop. Running the
# daemon by hand instead? Source that file first.

# Crush driven from Signal, on Z.ai's GLM-5.3 Flash, with permission prompts off.
#
# MODEL PINNING. Crush has no --model flag; the model comes from config, and the
# last config file wins (GlobalConfig → GlobalConfigData → project configs).
# CRUSH_GLOBAL_DATA, set in the env_file below, repoints GlobalConfigData at
# ~/.local/share/crush-signal/crush.json, which pins glm-5.3-flash on the zai provider
# for THIS harness only. The `crush` you run interactively keeps its own model —
# the shared ~/.config/crush/crush.json still pins nothing, by design.
#
# --yolo auto-accepts every permission prompt. That's the point of an agent you
# drive from your phone — nobody is at the keyboard to approve a bash call — and
# it's also exactly why `enabled = false`. Flip to true only if you want a
# permission-free agent live in ~/src from the moment you log in.
#
# --channels signal opts the `signal` MCP server into the fork's channel feature,
# so inbound Signal messages arrive as live events mid-session rather than only
# when a tool is polled. Opt-in is per-server and CLI-only (there is no config
# key for it — hence args), and both "signal" and "server:signal" are accepted.
#
# SIGNAL ONLY. This harness used to also carry `--channels switchboard`, which
# split webhook duty with the phone: doorbells landed in whatever session won
# the race, and the Switchboard queue looked dead because the events were being
# consumed by a Signal-driven agent nobody was watching. crush-switchboard below
# now owns the switchboard channel exclusively — do not re-add it here.
#
# PREFIX IS IDENTITY-CONDITIONAL — see crush-signal.env.tmpl, which renders it.
# On a human login (joestump, e.g. kitt) the Signal account is Joe's own and its
# Note to Self carries other automations' items, so the agent keeps the `cc`
# prefix. On an agent login (joestump-agent, e.g. tars) the account IS the
# agent's, every trusted-sender message is meant for it, and no prefix is set.
#
# Both boxes used to ship `cc`, which made the tars agent look dead: green in
# `harness list`, zero restarts, silent to every unprefixed message.
#
# Changing this is an env_file change, NOT an OpenBao one: harness appends
# env_file last (spawn.go buildEnv) and later duplicates shadow earlier ones, so
# an env_file line beats anything Vault Agent renders.
#
# RESTART = ON-FAILURE, NOT ALWAYS. This is the only harness here that costs
# money per launch, so it is the one that must not respawn into a wall. Two
# ways it differs from its Claude siblings:
#
#   * A clean exit means the agent was told to quit, and respawning it fights
#     whoever did that. Worse, per the note above, a clean exit clears the
#     consecutive-failure counter — so "always" turns an exit-0 loop into the
#     one loop the daemon's give-up rule can never break.
#   * Z.ai is quota-metered. When the quota goes, every launch fails at
#     stream-open; "on-failure" then walks the 6-attempt budget and parks in
#     `failed`, visible in `harness doctor` and spending nothing further.
#
# restart_delay = 30, up from 5. The budget is 6 attempts either way, so a
# longer delay buys no extra launches — it spreads them over minutes instead
# of seconds, which gives a transient blip (a Z.ai 5xx, a Signal reconnect)
# room to clear INSIDE the budget rather than burning it in half a minute. A
# failed launch is not free: it starts crush, loads every MCP server, and
# makes a provider request before it finds out.
[harness.crush-signal]
harness = "crush"
args = ["--yolo", "--channels", "signal"]
workdir = "{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/src"
env_file = "{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/.config/harness/crush-signal.env"
description = "Crush · GLM-5.3 Flash (Z.ai) · YOLO · Signal channel ({{ hasSuffix "-agent" (.agentIdentity | default .chezmoi.username) | ternary "no prefix" "cc prefix" }})"
restart = "on-failure"
restart_delay = 30
enabled = false

# The Switchboard worker: Crush sitting in ~/src, woken by webhook doorbells.
#
# --channels switchboard opts the `switchboard` MCP server into the fork's
# channel feature, so inbound todos (Gitea/GitHub webhook events, and anything
# else routed into a granted queue) arrive as live events mid-session instead
# of sitting unclaimed until something polls list_todos. Same opt-in mechanics
# as crush-signal's signal channel: per-server, CLI-only, no config key.
#
# NO SIGNAL CHANNEL here, by design. One channel consumer per server: the
# signal channel belongs to crush-signal, this one answers doorbells only.
# Alerts that need a human go out via the signal MCP's send tools, which work
# fine from a non-channel session.
#
# Same model-pinning and restart economics as crush-signal — see that block's
# comments. CRUSH_GLOBAL_DATA in the env file repoints this harness at its own
# ~/.local/share/crush-switchboard/crush.json (glm-5.2 / glm-5.3-flash on zai),
# which is chezmoi-managed and czu-reasserted exactly like crush-signal's — the
# model an unattended agent runs is declared config, and an unpinned harness
# silently takes crush's first-enabled-provider fallback while `harness list`
# still shows the model this description claims.
#
# @joestump-agent 08/26/2026 - Added, taking over the switchboard channel from
#   crush-signal so webhook events have exactly one always-on consumer.
#
# @joestump 08/29/2026 - Corrected the model-pin note during review of #197: the
#   pin is chezmoi-managed and czu-reasserted like crush-signal's, not seeded
#   per box. It shipped unpinned, which meant every machine but the one it was
#   hand-seeded on would have run crush's fallback model under this GLM-5.2
#   description.
[harness.crush-switchboard]
harness = "crush"
args = ["--yolo", "--channels", "switchboard"]
workdir = "{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/src"
env_file = "{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/.config/harness/crush-switchboard.env"
description = "Crush · GLM-5.3 Flash (Z.ai) · YOLO · Switchboard channel (webhook todos)"
restart = "on-failure"
restart_delay = 30
enabled = false

# Claude Code in ~/src, driven from the Claude app / claude.ai via Remote Control.
#
# --remote-control starts a normal interactive session and registers it for
# remote drive-by, so the phone is a second keyboard on THIS session rather than
# a separate cloud one: same working tree, same MCP servers, same ~/.claude.json.
# The session name is auto-generated from the hostname
# (--remote-control-session-name-prefix overrides that) — take the name from
# `harness logs claude-code` on first start.
#
# Deliberately NOT Signal-driven: the `signal` MCP in ~/.claude.json is wired
# WITHOUT --channel (see .chezmoiscripts/run_after_43-claude-code-mcp-merge.sh.tmpl),
# and crush-signal above already owns this box's Signal channel — on an agent
# login it answers every trusted-sender message with no prefix at all. A second
# Signal-channel agent there would answer the very same messages, so the human
# gets two replies to everything. If you ever do want a Signal-channel Claude,
# give it an env_file setting SIGNAL_MCP_CHANNEL=1 AND its own
# SIGNAL_MCP_PREFIX — signal-mcp reads both from the environment — and expect to
# give crush-signal a distinct prefix at the same time.
#
# --dangerously-skip-permissions is the claude equivalent of crush's --yolo, and
# carries the same consequence: `enabled = false`, start it by hand.
#
# harness = "claude-code" runs a bare `claude`, resolved against the daemon's
# PATH — and both service units put ~/.local/bin first, so this lands on the
# claude shim (a symlink into ~/.local/share/claude/versions/<v>) and a claude
# self-update is still picked up on the next restart without editing anything.
[harness.claude-code]
harness = "claude-code"
args = ["--remote-control", "--dangerously-skip-permissions"]
workdir = "{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/src"
description = "Claude Code · ~/src · Remote Control · skip-permissions"
restart = "always"
restart_delay = 5
enabled = false

# The always-on background worker: Claude Code sitting in ~/src waiting for
# Switchboard doorbells and queue todos. Replaces the standalone
# claude-headless.service + tmux while-loop the zsh-harnessd era used
# (retired by run_onchange_after_51-harness-daemon-service).
#
# Deliberately NOT Signal-driven and NOT remote-control: claude-code above owns
# the phone, crush-signal owns the Signal channel; this one just works the
# queue. Same --dangerously-skip-permissions consequence as its siblings:
# `enabled = false`, start it deliberately with `harness start claude-headless`.
[harness.claude-headless]
harness = "claude-code"
args = ["--dangerously-skip-permissions"]
workdir = "{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/src"
description = "Claude Code · ~/src · headless queue worker · skip-permissions"
restart = "always"
restart_delay = 5
enabled = false

# Scheduled harnesses — one drop-in file each in harness.d/
#
# One-shot prompt harnesses fired by the daemon's own cron scheduler live in
# ~/.config/harness/harness.d/*.toml (see [server] harness_d below, wired by
# stump.wtf/harness PR #227): the 6h StumpCloud health sweep (formerly the
# standalone systemd timer / launchd agent, retired by
# run_onchange_after_52-harness-reload.sh.tmpl) plus the daily PR-feedback
# sweep and weekly issue/PR grooming (formerly never-actually-scheduled
# ~/.claude/scheduled-tasks definitions).
#
# Drop-in rules: a harness.d file may contain ONLY [harness.*] tables —
# [server], [profile.*] and [daemon] are rejected with source-located errors
# — duplicate names across files or with this config are rejected, non-.toml
# files are skipped, and files load in lexicographic order. So the set of
# scheduled tasks is exactly the set of files in harness.d/: add or retire a
# schedule by adding or deleting one file, never by editing this template.
#
# `schedule` is validated at config load: it requires `prompt`, is mutually
# exclusive with `enabled = true` and with profile membership, and only allows
# restart "no"/"on-failure". Instructions live in chezmoi-managed prompt files,
# so a prompt edit propagates with a normal czu and re-fires the reload script
# via its embedded hash (which now hashes the drop-ins too). Each drop-in is
# gated on the `-agent` login suffix (the same gate the old inline block here
# carried) — human logins render the drop-ins EMPTY, which is valid TOML and
# contributes nothing, so only agent boxes run sweeps.
#
# @joestump-agent 08/18/2026 - Added the three scheduled harnesses inline
#   here, replacing the standalone stumpcloud-sweep timer/launchd pair and
#   the dormant ~/.claude/scheduled-tasks definitions.
#
# @joestump-agent 08/19/2026 - Moved them out to harness.d/ drop-ins per the
#   harness_d pattern; schedules and prompts unchanged.

# ── Profiles ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Named sets that `harness use-profile <name>` switches between. These existed
# in the rendered copies on real machines but never in this template, because
# create_ meant the template stopped being the source of truth the moment a
# machine was seeded. They are declared here so the set is the same everywhere.
#
# autostart marks which profile the daemon brings up on start. Exactly one
# should carry it — see stump.wtf/harness#99 for what happens when the daemon's
# persisted active profile does not resolve (it silently starts nothing).
[profile.default]
description = "Everyday set — Crush Signal channel + Crush Switchboard worker + headless Claude worker"
harnesses = ["crush-signal", "crush-switchboard", "claude-headless"]
autostart = true

[profile.full]
description = "All harnesses running"
harnesses = ["crush-signal", "crush-switchboard", "claude-code", "claude-headless"]
autostart = false

# Remote access — the SSH cockpit
#
# The daemon can expose its cockpit over SSH (Wish, ADR-0004; public-key only,
# no password path, ADR-0008) so a thin client on a phone or laptop can attach:
# `ssh -p <port> <host>` lands in the full TUI dashboard. The username ssh asks
# for is irrelevant — Wish never checks it; the key is the only credential.
#
# BOTH knobs of this surface live in the OpenBao bag secret/users/$USER/harness
# — never in this committed template:
#
#   harness_authorized_keys  the public key(s) allowed to attach. Rendered to
#                            ~/.ssh/harness_authorized_keys by Vault Agent
#                            (harness-ssh.ctmpl writes every *_authorized_keys
#                            field of the bag to ~/.ssh/<field>). Only the
#                            joestump@ key belongs there — this is a
#                            full-control (typing) surface on every box. A
#                            machine whose bag has no such field has no
#                            allow-list file and the listener accepts no one:
#                            safe by default.
#   HARNESS_SSH_PORT         the port, exported as an env var by
#                            secrets-static.env.ctmpl (every field of every
#                            non-ssh/aws bag is exported verbatim) and read
#                            here at apply time.
#
#   vault kv put secret/users/$USER/harness \
#     harness_authorized_keys=@id_ed25519.pub HARNESS_SSH_PORT=23234
#
# The default below is NOT env gating — the banned pattern (dotfiles#106) was an
# if-env block that renders a file EMPTY when the var is absent. This always
# emits a valid listen line, and the default is upstream's own: unprivileged
# (>1024), so the user-level daemon binds it with no root.
#
# But the default applies on ANY render whose shell lacks the secret env, not
# just a fresh box mid-bootstrap: czu sources secrets-static.env before
# applying, a bare `chezmoi apply` from a non-interactive shell (an agent's,
# say) does not. So if you set HARNESS_SSH_PORT to anything OTHER than the
# default, the rendered port flaps between the two across applies, re-firing
# run_onchange_after_52 each time. Keep bao's value equal to the default below
# unless you have a reason not to, and change both together if you do.
#
# Changing the port takes effect on a daemon RESTART, not on the next czu:
# `harness reload` re-reads harness definitions only, never the [server]
# listener (see run_onchange_after_52-harness-reload.sh.tmpl).
#
# @joestump-agent 08/19/2026 - Enabled the SSH cockpit, allow-list and port
#   both sourced from the OpenBao secret/users/$USER/harness bag.
#
# @joestump 08/19/2026 - Review pass: fixed the mangled `vault kv put` example
#   (the line-continuation had been replaced with a `#`), and corrected two
#   wrong claims above — the port default applies on any apply without the
#   secret env, not only mid-bootstrap, and the listener needs a daemon restart
#   rather than the reload script.
[server]
enabled = true
listen = "0.0.0.0:{{ env "HARNESS_SSH_PORT" | default "23234" }}"
authorized_keys_file = "{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/.ssh/harness_authorized_keys"
# Drop-in directory for [harness.*] definitions (stump.wtf/harness PR #227):
# every *.toml in it loads after this file and its harness tables merge in.
# The scheduled one-shots live there — one file per schedule — so the set of
# scheduled tasks is managed file-by-file, not by editing this template.
# Unlike the SSH listener above, this one does NOT need a daemon restart:
# the drop-in load happens inside config.Parse, so every `harness reload`
# re-reads both this path and the whole directory. Adding, editing or deleting
# a drop-in takes effect on the next reload — which is what
# run_onchange_after_52-harness-reload.sh.tmpl already fires.
#
# The directory must EXIST, though: harness_d pointing at a missing directory
# is a hard config-load error that takes the whole file down. It exists because
# every drop-in renders its comment header unconditionally, so chezmoi always
# writes the files (a template rendering to nothing is REMOVED, not written).
harness_d = "{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/.config/harness/harness.d"
